Team,

Attaching the decision tree we reviewed for DOP-207 / FAQ-QS-01. Please route=
 all new QuickSend claims through this logic effective immediately.

The key call is still source of initiation, not whether the customer was pres=
sured. If the fraudster logged in and sent the transfer using credentials or =
an OTP, it is Reg E unauthorized. If the customer used their own app session =
and sent the payment to a bad actor, we do not classify it as unauthorized, b=
ut we still log the matter for scam review and possible reimbursement conside=
ration.

- If a fraudster obtained the customer's QuickSend login credentials or one-t=
ime passcode and initiated the payment, the payment is treated as an unauthor=
ized transfer under Regulation E, even if the customer was tricked into shari=
ng the credentials.
- If the customer personally sent a QuickSend payment to a scammer, the payme=
nt is not an unauthorized transfer, but the claim is still logged and reviewe=
d under the Scam Reimbursement Guideline.

Tasha, please make sure Claims Intake is using the updated intake notes. Greg=
, can you have QA spot-check the first batch next week? We need consistency w=
ith the 3/1 CFPB FAQ response and no drift back to the old =E2=80=9Cscam =3D =
unauthorized=E2=80=9D shorthand.

Luis

